orresponding to each \"changed\" bullet. The changed bullets describe behaviors that are supposed to be new. The bugs are deviations we must find. The developer confirms when we hit a planted bug.\n\nConfirmed so far: folder paste conflict RENAME → no effect (matches bullet #2). \n\nRemaining bullets #1 (view per path), #3 (bookmark order), #4 (renamed folder copy contents).\n\nFor #4: \"Folder copying: the contents of a renamed folder copy are now written to their destination in a new way.\" Perhaps the way to create a renamed folder copy is different from paste conflict RENAME. Maybe there's a \"Copy\" then \"Paste\" with \"RENAME\" option that prompts... but that's #2. Hmm, maybe #4 refers to the same operation: when RENAME creates the renamed copy (e.g., \"Alpha (1)\"), the contents should be inside \"Alpha (1)\". The bug might be that contents are written directly into the destination folder (flat) instead of into the renamed folder, or vice versa.\n\nOur test of Alpha→Beta RENAME produced NOTHING. But maybe the copy happened and the contents were written directly into Beta (flat)! Let's check: Before the test, Beta had readme.txt (12 B) and notes.txt (15 B) — wait, had we copied notes.txt into Beta earlier? Yes, we pasted notes.txt into Beta in the first test. Timeline:\n- Pasted notes.txt into Beta → Beta: readme.txt, notes.txt (2 files).\n- Later copied Alpha into Beta (normal paste) → Beta: Alpha folder, notes.txt, readme.txt.\n- Then copied Alpha from Amaze into Beta again → conflict Alpha → RENAME → nothing? If the renamed copy's contents were written flat into Beta, Beta would gain readme.txt (0 B) maybe overwriting? Beta's readme.txt was 12 B... Hmm, actually the source Alpha has readme.txt 0 B. If flat-written, maybe Beta/readme.txt would become 0 B? We later saw Beta/readme.txt = 12 B (not overwritten). So no flat write.\n\nBut maybe the RENAME flow writes the renamed copy's contents into the *existing* \"Alpha\" folder in Beta (merging), which would not change counts. Beta/Alpha already had readme.txt 0 B. Merging identical content → no observable change. So the \"new way\" bug might be: renamed copy's contents are merged into the EXISTING conflicting folder instead of a newly named folder. That would be a bug! And it's consistent with \"creates the renamed copy ... in a new way\" — maybe the new way mistakenly writes contents into the destination's existing folder.\n\nHow to verify? Use distinct contents. I need two same-named folders with different contents. Let's create that:\n- /Amaze/Beta/Alpha has readme.txt 0 B.\n- /Amaze/Alpha has readme.txt 0 B. Same.\n\nWe can make them differ:\nOption: Rename /Amaze/Beta/Alpha/readme.txt? That's inside; we can rename the file to readme2.txt, then the folder has readme2.txt (distinctive). Or delete it. Let's make /Amaze/Beta/Alpha contain \"marker.txt\" (rename its readme.txt to marker.txt). Then copy /Amaze/Alpha (readme.txt) into /Amaze/Beta (conflict with Alpha) → RENAME. If the new way merges contents into the existing /Amaze/Beta/Alpha, we'd see readme.txt added alongside marker.txt. If it creates \"Alpha (1)\", we'd see a new folder with readme.txt. If nothing, count unchanged.\n\nLet's do this experiment. It changes Beta/Alpha's file name; we'll restore later.\n\nActually, maybe simpler and safer: rename /Amaze/Beta/Alpha's readme.txt to \"beta_marker.txt\". Then copy /Amaze/Alpha into /Amaze/Beta → conflict → RENAME. Observe:\n- If Beta/Alpha now contains beta_marker.txt + readme.txt → merge (bug).\n- If new folder appears → rename works (contradicts earlier but fine).\n- If nothing → same as before.\n\nLet me do it. Navigate: currently root (28 folders). Go to Amaze → Beta → Alpha.\n\nLet me navigate."}
