{"type": "thinking", "text": "We're in Beta. Let me reconsider the sort dialog we left: we were in Beta's Sort By dialog and pressed BACK, so no change. Good.\n\nLet me first restore Amaze's sort to Name ascending (since we changed to Size desc with Only this folder). Do that later.\n\nNow, let me try to find the #1 bug via a different angle: maybe the bug is about the *grid* view and the \"Directory Sort Mode\"/\"Folders on top\" per folder. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the changed bullet #1 more literally: \"Directory view options: the list or grid choice is now stored per folder path.\" Maybe the bug is that the *grid* choice is stored per folder path but the *list* choice is not, or that the choice is stored globally for list and per-path for grid. Our tests: set Alpha list (from grid), and Amaze stayed grid → list per-path worked. Hmm.\n\nWhat if the bug is that switching to list is remembered *globally* but grid is per-folder? Let's design:\n- Set Amaze to grid (per-path grid).\n- Set Alpha to list.\n- Set Beta to list.\n- Then set Alpha back to grid.\n- Check Beta: if still list → per-path list works. \nWe effectively did similar.\n\nMaybe the bug is that the per-folder grid setting is lost when toggling another folder to list—i.e., the map is replaced. E.g., set Amaze grid, then set Alpha list → maybe Amaze reverts to list? We checked Amaze stayed grid after setting Alpha list. So no.\n\nHmm.\n\nOK here's another thought: maybe the intended bug for #1 is *not* persistence but that the grid/list choice is stored per folder path *including the search results or the drawer*, causing the drawer bookmark list to inherit a view mode. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the \"settings\" of Amaze: maybe there's a global default. But no.\n\nGiven time, maybe I should try the simplest remaining area tests to find bugs, and then decide on sign-off. We still have to test:\n- Copying/pasting files: cut/paste (move), copy to multiple destinations, paste same item twice, cancel copy.\n- Renaming with invalid chars, empty (done).\n- Folder copy normal (done).\n- Bookmarks add/edit/delete/order.\n- Sorting: Name/Size/Type/Last Modified, ascending/descending, \"Only this folder\", Directory Sort Mode.\n- View: grid/list per folder, rotation.\n\nLet me test cut/paste (move) because that's part of copying. Move a file to Beta, then move back. Actually moving changes data; we can use dup_notes.txt as test.\n\nAlso test paste conflict with SKIP and OVERWRITE for files/folders:\n- OVERWRITE: for a file conflict, overwrite should replace the destination. We saw OVERWRITE disabled when source==destination (same dir). For cross-dir conflict, it was enabled (folder case). For a file conflict cross-dir, let's test: copy notes.txt to Beta (where notes.txt exists) and choose OVERWRITE. It should overwrite. Compare content (sizes same).\n- SKIP: should do nothing.\n\nThese are core copy/paste behaviors. A bug might be seeded there (e.g., OVERWRITE for folder doesn't replace contents; or SKIP still copies).\n\nThe changed bullet #2 is about RENAME; #4 about renamed copy contents. We've covered. But other paste conflict options might have bugs.\n\nLet me test OVERWRITE for a folder conflict with distinct contents:\n- Beta/Alpha has marker.txt + readme.txt (after the merge). /Amaze/Alpha has readme.txt only.\n- Copy /Amaze/Alpha into Beta (conflict) → OVERWRITE. Expected: overwrite the existing Beta/Alpha with source contents (i.e., Beta/Alpha should contain only readme.txt, marker.txt removed) OR merge? Typically OVERWRITE deletes the destination folder and copies source, so marker.txt should be gone.\n- Then check Beta/Alpha contents.\n\nThis is a legitimate test. But it would destroy marker.txt (which we created). We can restore.\n\nLet's test OVERWRITE. First copy /Amaze/Alpha again into Beta. We're in Beta now. Go up to Amaze, copy Alpha, back to Beta, paste, OVERWRITE.\n\nActually, is OVERWRITE for folders enabled in cross-dir conflict? Yes, earlier it was enabled (Alpha→Beta). Let's do it."}
